; ============================================================================
; A POSTURE RECORDS A KEY, NOT AN ADDRESS -- R130
; ============================================================================
;
; WHAT THIS PROVES, AND IN WHICH ORDER IT WAS WRITTEN.
;
; This file was written BEFORE the fix and run BEFORE the fix, which is the
; order R128 and AIF-137 both used and the order that caught a false green in
; each. Read the arm table below as the prediction it was; the closeout says
; what actually happened.
;
; THE SUBJECT. workspace_load_from_stream() replays the AREA numbers a posture
; recorded as ENGINE ADDRESSES -- `AREA 0` becomes open_into_area(0, ...),
; which calls get_area_0based(0) and A.close() on that exact slot. That can
; only be safe if slot 0 is free, which workspace_close_all() at
; cmd_workspace.cpp:2405 guaranteed by emptying every workspace in the
; session. R130 rules the recorded number a KEY: on load, slots come from
; cli::find_free_area_for_current_workspace(), and a map from recorded key to
; allocated slot is consulted by the CURSOR and CURRENT handlers.
;
; THE MAP IS THE HALF THE ARMS ARE AIMED AT. Scoping the close without the map
; is WORSE than the defect being fixed: the loader would allocate past the
; occupied range, and then a bare `CURSOR 0 4` would still reach engine slot 0
; -- now another workspace's table -- and MOVE ITS CURSOR. That is AIF-137's
; shape as a WRITE rather than a read, driven by a saved file. Every arm below
; goes RED under that implementation, which is why there are five of them.
;
; THE THREE WORLDS EACH ARM IS READ IN:
;
;   TODAY        close_all empties everything, then PKBT lands at slot 0 and
;                PKBU at slot 1. Workspace A is gone.
;   NO MAP       A survives at slots 0 and 1, PKBT/PKBU allocate to 2 and 3,
;                but CURSOR 0 / CURSOR 1 / CURRENT 0 still address 0 and 1 --
;                so A's cursors are DRIVEN and the loaded tables are left at
;                row 1.
;   R130         A survives untouched at 0 and 1; PKBT/PKBU at 2 and 3 with
;                their recorded cursors restored through the map.
;
;   arm   reads                          TODAY      NO MAP     R130
;   ----  -----------------------------  ---------  ---------  ------
;   T5    engine current, no SELECT      .T. WRONG  .F.        .T.
;   T1    slot 0                         .F.        .F.        .T.
;   T2    slot 1                         .F.        .F.        .T.
;   T3    PKBT by name                   .T. WRONG  .F.        .T.
;   T4    PKBU by name                   .T. WRONG  .F.        .T.
;
; T3, T4 and T5 ARE GREEN TODAY FOR THE WRONG REASON AND ARE MARKED AS SUCH.
; With the slot space emptied first, the recorded number and the allocated slot
; coincide by accident. They do not discriminate against today's build; they
; discriminate against a NEW implementation whose map is wrong, which is the
; failure this ruling exists to prevent. A green T3 on today's binary is not
; evidence of anything and must not be reported as one.
;
; T1 AND T2 ARE THE ARMS THAT CAN GO RED TODAY, and they were built so they
; cannot go BLANK instead. Both fixture families use the SAME FIELD NAME `LBL`
; with DIFFERENT VALUES, so slot 0 and slot 1 are readable in all three worlds
; and the marker never errors. An errored marker in this language PRINTS
; NOTHING rather than going red, so an arm that would read a CLOSED area is an
; arm that silently disappears; sharing the field name is what stops that.
;
; NOTHING HERE ASSERTS AN ABSENCE. No marker in this language can, and none of
; these tries to. Every arm is a positive read of LBL against a specific value.
;
; ADDRESSING: SLOTS FOR THE SURVIVORS, NAMES FOR THE TRAVELLERS -- AND THE
; DIFFERENCE FROM relation_parent_workspace_crossing.dts IS DELIBERATE.
; That spec forbade name addressing because its two fixtures shared names by
; design, so a name could not say which table it read. HERE THE FOUR TABLE
; NAMES ARE UNIQUE, so `SELECT PKBT` is unambiguous -- and it is the only way
; to ask the question T3 asks, which is "wherever the loaded table went, is its
; cursor where the posture said". A slot-addressed T3 would have to name a slot,
; and the whole point is that the slot changes.
;
; GUARDS. PK_G0a..G0d prove each fixture on its own before any workspace
; exists. PK_G1a/G1b prove workspace B landed in slots 0 and 1 BEFORE the save,
; because that is what makes the posture record keys 0 and 1 -- if B landed
; elsewhere the posture says something else and every arm below is meaningless.
; PK_G2a/G2b prove workspace A occupies slots 0 and 1 and is parked on the rows
; the arms expect. IF ANY GUARD READS .F., TREAT EVERY ARM AS UNPROVEN RATHER
; THAN AS FAILING.
;
; THE ALPHABETICAL SLOT ORDER IS ASSERTED, NOT ASSUMED. A directory open sorts
; its tables, so PKAT precedes PKAU and PKBT precedes PKBU. G1 and G2 read a
; field in each slot to prove it rather than trusting the sort.
;
; SET PATH ORDERING IS A KNOWN TRAP IN THIS FILE'S SHAPE. workspace_additive_
; open.dts was once edited so a SET PATH landed BEFORE the previous table's
; CREATE, and a fixture was built in the wrong directory. Every CREATE and
; every ERASE below is immediately preceded by the SET PATH that owns it.
;
; THE POSTURE FILE pk_posture.dtschema IS WRITTEN INTO THE WORKSPACES ROOT and
; is left there, overwritten on each run. Prior art: keyregr_sandbox.dtschema
; from key_metadata_regression.dts sits in the same directory for the same
; reason. Fixtures in DBF/SANDBOX/PKA and DBF/SANDBOX/PKB are erased at the
; end; all three workspaces are destroyed in teardown (D10.3) so the NAMES are
; freed and a later run mints fresh rather than adopting. The catalog ROWS
; remain -- that is history by design.
; ============================================================================

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE DESTROY PKWSA
WORKSPACE DESTROY PKWSB
WORKSPACE DESTROY PKWSC

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/PKA
ERASE pkat CONFIRM
ERASE pkau CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/PKB
ERASE pkbt CONFIRM
ERASE pkbu CONFIRM

; --- fixtures: four tables, unique names, one shared field name -------------
; LBL is shared so every slot is readable in every world (see header).
; The VALUES are what say which table a read came from.

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/PKA
SELECT 0
CREATE X64 PKAT (LBL C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "A_1"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "A_2"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "A_3"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "A_4"

SELECT 1
CREATE X64 PKAU (LBL C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "AU_1"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "AU_2"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "AU_3"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "AU_4"

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/PKB
SELECT 2
CREATE X64 PKBT (LBL C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "B_1"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "B_2"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "B_3"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "B_4"

SELECT 3
CREATE X64 PKBU (LBL C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "BU_1"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "BU_2"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "BU_3"
APPEND
REPLACE LBL WITH "BU_4"

SELECT 0
GO 2
? "PK_G0a_PKAT_fixture_written:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "A_2")
SELECT 1
GO 3
? "PK_G0b_PKAU_fixture_written:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "AU_3")
SELECT 2
GO 4
? "PK_G0c_PKBT_fixture_written:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "B_4")
SELECT 3
GO 2
? "PK_G0d_PKBU_fixture_written:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "BU_2")

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL

; --- phase 1: build the posture from workspace B, and from B ALONE ----------
; Bare SAVE is scoped to the current workspace (R128), so this posture holds
; PKBT and PKBU and nothing else. The cursors parked here are what CURSOR
; lines record; the SELECT before the save is what CURRENT records.

WORKSPACE OPEN DBF/SANDBOX/PKB AS PKWSB

SELECT 0
GO 4
? "PK_G1a_posture_source_slot0_is_PKBT:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "B_4")
SELECT 1
GO 2
? "PK_G1b_posture_source_slot1_is_PKBU:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "BU_2")

SELECT 0
WORKSPACE SAVE pk_posture V3

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE DESTROY PKWSB

; --- phase 2: workspace A takes the low slots the posture names -------------
; This is the arrangement the whole file exists to create: the numbers the
; posture recorded now belong to somebody else.

WORKSPACE OPEN DBF/SANDBOX/PKA AS PKWSA

SELECT 0
GO 2
? "PK_G2a_A_occupies_slot0_parked_on_A_2:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "A_2")
SELECT 1
GO 3
? "PK_G2b_A_occupies_slot1_parked_on_AU_3:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "AU_3")

; --- phase 3: load the posture into a THIRD workspace -----------------------
; NEW declares; SWITCH is what makes the next opened area join it. NEW does
; not switch on its own and its own output says so.

WORKSPACE NEW PKWSC
WORKSPACE SWITCH PKWSC
WORKSPACE LOAD pk_posture

; --- PK_T5: CURRENT landed on the LOADED table ------------------------------
; No SELECT since the load, deliberately: this arm reads whatever the loader
; left selected. GREEN TODAY FOR THE WRONG REASON (slot 0 happens to hold the
; loaded table). Under a missing map it reads A_4, because CURRENT 0 would
; select workspace A's slot 0 -- which the missing map has also just driven.

? "PK_T5_current_selected_the_loaded_table:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "B_4")

; --- PK_T1 / PK_T2: the other workspace survived, and was NOT driven --------
; One arm, two claims, and that is intentional: "still open" and "still where
; I parked it" fail together under today's close and fail together under a
; missing map, so separating them would buy nothing and cost two more markers.
; TODAY these read B_4 and BU_2 -- the loaded tables sitting in A's slots.
; UNDER A MISSING MAP they read A_4 and AU_2 -- A's own tables, driven by the
; posture's CURSOR lines. Both are .F., and the VALUE PRINTED IS NOT VISIBLE
; from the marker, so the closeout must say which world was observed.

SELECT 0
? "PK_T1_other_workspace_slot0_undisturbed:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "A_2")
SELECT 1
? "PK_T2_other_workspace_slot1_undisturbed:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "AU_3")

; --- PK_T3 / PK_T4: the loaded cursors were restored, wherever they landed --
; By NAME, because the slot is exactly what this ruling makes unpredictable.
; The four names are unique, so the name is unambiguous here in a way it was
; not in relation_parent_workspace_crossing.dts. GREEN TODAY FOR THE WRONG
; REASON; .F. under a missing map, where both tables sit at row 1.

SELECT PKBT
? "PK_T3_loaded_first_table_cursor_restored:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "B_4")
SELECT PKBU
? "PK_T4_loaded_second_table_cursor_restored:" + (ALLTRIM(LBL) = "BU_2")

; --- teardown ---------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE DESTROY PKWSA
WORKSPACE DESTROY PKWSB
WORKSPACE DESTROY PKWSC

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/PKA
ERASE pkat CONFIRM
ERASE pkau CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/PKB
ERASE pkbt CONFIRM
ERASE pkbu CONFIRM

SET PATH DBF DBF
